": {"margin": 1.82, "lean": -0.75, "environment": 4.15, "incumbency": 0.0, "money": -1.58}, "rating_margin": 0.71, "sigma_race": 5.5, "sigma_total": 5.9, "baseline_margin": 0.06, "analyst_adjustment": -0.46, "key_factors": ["Polls since September consistently show Jackson ahead by 1-3 points", "Jackson's massive fundraising edge: about $119M raised to Bottoms's $6M", "Open seat with a midterm environment favoring Democrats", "Raters nearly unanimous Tossup; prediction markets split"], "rationale": "Polling from mid-September through mid-October consistently shows Jackson narrowly ahead and published averages put the race at roughly Jackson plus two, while his fundraising advantage is enormous. A Democratic-tilted midterm environment, the open seat, and a near-unanimous Tossup consensus among raters offset that edge, leaving the race effectively even with a slight Republican tilt.", "watch": "Turnout dynamics in the Atlanta suburbs and among Black voters, amplified by Obama's late visit, or a continued drift toward Jackson in polling could tip the narrow edge either way.", "confidence": "low", "notes": ["Open seat: Brian Kemp (R) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Georgia's open governor's race sits at the center of the national battlefield, with former Atlanta Mayor Keisha Lance Bottoms trying to flip the office for Democrats after Brian Kemp's departure. Recent surveys from mid-September through mid-October show Rick Jackson ahead by one to three points, and published averages put the race at roughly Jackson plus two. Prediction markets disagree with one another, pricing the contest between even and a slight Republican edge.\n\nThe fundamentals cut both ways: Jackson holds an enormous fundraising advantage, having raised about $119 million to Bottoms's $6 million, but the midterm environment favors the out-party and nearly all race raters call the state a tossup. With the seat open, polling thin, and most surveys sponsored by Republican-leaning outlets, this re